    "issue": "Scraped HTML doesn't match what you see in the browser",
    "fix": "The page may be rendered with JavaScript after the initial load, which a plain requests.get() call never sees -- it only gets the HTML the server sends before any script runs. Use your browser's 'View Page Source' (not Inspect, which shows the post-JavaScript version) to check the price is really there before picking a target for this project."
  },
  {
    "issue": "Getting blocked or a 403 after a few checks",
    "fix": "Keep the BROWSER_HEADER User-Agent, add a short delay between requests if you're checking several targets in a row, and don't poll the same page more than a few times an hour. Most sites tolerate occasional polite requests but block obvious hammering."
  },
  {
    "issue": "Getting an alert on every run even though nothing really changed",
    "fix": "You're likely comparing text with different whitespace or formatting each run (e.g. a trailing space that a live page adds sometimes). scrape_product() already strips this with get_text(strip=True) -- if you add your own scraping code elsewhere, make sure it cleans values the same way before saving a snapshot."
  },
  {
    "issue": "The AI's alert doesn't come back in the [SUMMARY]/[DETAILS] format you asked for",
    "fix": "This is an expected AI-reliability failure, not a bug in your code: AI replies aren't guaranteed to follow formatting instructions exactly. parse_alert() returns an empty dict when it can't find the markers, and summarize_changes() falls back to using the AI's raw reply as the details text instead of silently dropping the alert. If you see the summary 'Competitor changes detected' in the output, that's the fallback firing -- the change was still caught, just not summarized as cleanly."
  },
  {
    "issue": "The scheduled check silently stops running",
    "fix": "If it's running on your own laptop, the schedule pauses the moment your laptop sleeps or shuts down -- cron and Task Scheduler can't run while the machine is off. For monitoring that keeps working while you're not watching, deploy it to a free always-on host the same way as the WhatsApp/Gmail course builds."
  }
